;; Regulatory source register for JPN-MHLW labour-standards compliance
;; (労働基準法 / 労働安全衛生法 and the 36協定 filing this repository is about).
;;
;; README.md says: every regulatory claim must cite the official MHLW source.
;; docs/business-model.md routes a legal conclusion the governor has not
;; cleared to human sign-off.
;;
;; Until this file existed there was no such source. The documents required a
;; citation against a set that did not exist, so nothing in this repository
;; could tell a real citation from an invented one. This is that set.
;;
;; Shape is tx-data (a vector of entity maps), like every other EDN corpus in
;; this workspace, so it loads with
;;   (d/transact conn (edn/read-string (slurp "facts.edn")))
;; :source/id is the join key. A regulation not in this table has NO
;; spec-basis for this repository -- extend the table, never invent an id, a
;; law id, or a URL.
;;
;; VERIFIED BY `nbb scripts/verify-facts.cljk`, which re-fetches every entry
;; from the live authority. Each entry carries a :source/verify tag naming
;; WHICH check establishes it. The checks are NOT interchangeable, and the
;; reasons are the main thing this file has to teach.
;;
;; Everything below was measured against these hosts on 2026-08-27. The
;; sibling registers in this cohort are written against other agencies and
;; their host notes DO NOT transfer -- this one is www.mhlw.go.jp and
;; laws.e-gov.go.jp, and each is wrong in its own way.
;;
;; A note on measuring instruments, because it went wrong twice while this
;; file was being written.
;;
;; First, bytes against characters: the same 404 response is 48546 bytes and
;; 42503 characters once decoded, and two passes that did not say which they
;; were counting looked like the host being unstable. It is not -- refetching
;; gives a byte-identical body with a stable sha256.
;;
;; Second, and this one actually corrupted the register: the header's text
;; lengths were produced by a throwaway script, and verify-facts.cljs was
;; written afterwards with its own tag stripper. The two differed only in how
;; they collapse whitespace around removed elements, and the 404 body came out
;; 3576 characters one way and 4389 the other. Same page, same day, same
;; fetch. Every character count here was wrong by about 23%, four pages that
;; are shorter than the 404 were listed as three, and NOTHING IN EITHER FILE
;; COULD HAVE SAID SO -- each was internally consistent.
;;
;; So there is now one instrument, in scripts/host_probe.cljk. Every number
;; below says which unit it is in and is regenerated by
;;   nbb --classpath scripts scripts/measure-host.cljk
;; which calls the same de-tag that verify-facts.cljs subtracts needles with.
;; If you change de-tag, rerun measure-host and update these numbers.
;;
;; -- THE OBVIOUS NEEDLE FOR THIS REPOSITORY IS ON THE 404 PAGE
;;
;; Occurrences on the fetched 404 body, tags and script elements removed,
;; measured 2026-08-27:
;;
;;   労働基準法          1
;;   労働基準監督署        2
;;   監督署            2
;;   届出             2
;;   労働安全衛生法        0
;;   36協定           0
;;   時間外労働          0
;;   就業規則           0
;;   割増賃金           0
;;
;; 労働基準法 and 労働基準監督署 are the first two phrases anyone would reach
;; for to prove an MHLW labour-standards page is the page they wanted. Both
;; are carried in this host's global navigation, so both are on a page that
;; does not exist. A citation checked with either of them verifies against a
;; deleted page.
;;
;; 労働安全衛生法 is NOT on the 404, which is why the safety page below is
;; allowed to use it and the labour-standards pages are not. The set is not
;; guessable from how central a term feels to the subject -- it is a property
;; of this host's chrome. Needles here are therefore chosen by SUBTRACTING the
;; live 404 body, and the verifier redoes that subtraction on every run: a
;; needle found on the fetched 404 REFUSES rather than passes.
;;
;; -- SIZE CANNOT IDENTIFY A DOCUMENT ON THIS HOST
;;
;; Measured 2026-08-27, response bytes:
;;
;;   /index.html                              200  84928 B  sha c2e00752f40a
;;   /stf/.../roudoukijun/index.html          200  84928 B  sha d6c1f7aa0d57
;;   /zzz-no-such-page-9f3c2a1b8e/            404  48546 B  sha e2c76c51ea5d
;;
;; The front page and the 労働基準 landing page were DIFFERENT DOCUMENTS of
;; EXACTLY THE SAME LENGTH. Each was refetched three times, each was stable
;; across those fetches, and the shas differ.
;;
;; About an hour later the same labour-standards page was 84639 bytes. The
;; coincidence was real and is already gone -- these landing pages carry dated
;; news items, so their length moves while the thing this register cites them
;; for does not.
;;
;; That second observation is the durable one and it is the stronger of the
;; two: on this host a CHANGED byte count does not mean the citation changed,
;; and for at least an hour an UNCHANGED one did not mean the page was the
;; same. Nothing below asserts a size, and the two host checks that do compare
;; lengths compare them against each other rather than against a pinned
;; number.
;;
;; The 404 is smaller than the front page here, unlike the sibling FSA host
;; where it is larger. That is not the reassurance it looks like. In TEXT the
;; 404 body is 4389 characters and FOUR of the pages this register depends on
;; are shorter than that:
;;
;;   working-hours guideline        3812   (577 shorter than the 404)
;;   forms index                    3834   (555 shorter)
;;   inspection-office directory    3880   (509 shorter)
;;   model work rules               4090   (299 shorter)
;;
;; A did-I-get-a-substantial-body heuristic passes the missing page and doubts
;; four real ones.
;;
;; -- HEAD NEVER REPORTS THE SIZE OF THE BODY YOU WILL GET
;;
;; Measured 2026-08-27, both with and without Accept-Encoding: gzip:
;;
;;                                        no gzip        gzip
;;   HEAD /zzz-no-such-page-9f3c2a1b8e/   CL: 0          CL: 0
;;   GET  /zzz-no-such-page-9f3c2a1b8e/   48546 bytes    48546 bytes
;;   HEAD /stf/.../roudoukijun/index.html no CL header   CL: 17143 (gzip)
;;   GET  /stf/.../roudoukijun/index.html 84928 bytes    84928 bytes
;;
;; The two live-page numbers drift together as that page's news items change
;; -- an hour later they were 17014 and 84639 -- so read the RELATION, not the
;; values. The missing page claims an EMPTY body and then serves 48546 bytes.
;; The live page reports no length at all under identity encoding, and under
;; gzip reports the compressed length, never the length the caller gets. So
;; the cheap liveness check, HEAD and look at Content-Length, reports the
;; missing page as empty and cannot size a live one in either direction. Every
;; check below issues a GET and reads the body, and the host check compares
;; the HEAD length against that run's own GET length rather than against
;; anything pinned here.
;;
;; The first draft of this section claimed something narrower and wrong: that
;; live pages send no Content-Length. They do, under gzip. That was a property
;; of the client used to measure it -- curl -I does not offer gzip and the
;; verifier's fetch does -- and it survived into this register until the host
;; check disagreed with it on the first run. A claim that only holds for the
;; client that measured it is not a claim about the host.
;;
;; -- THE REPEAL FIELDS ARE NOT IN THE OBJECT THEY LOOK LIKE THEY ARE IN
;;
;; On laws.e-gov.go.jp each result has two sibling objects, law_info and
;; revision_info. repeal_status, repeal_date, remain_in_force and
;; current_revision_status are all in revision_info. law_info holds only the
;; law_id, number, type and promulgation date.
;;
;; Read repeal_status from law_info -- the object named for the law, which is
;; where it reads as if it belongs -- and you get nil. The live value for a
;; law that is NOT repealed is the string None. A nil and a None are one
;; keystroke apart in a log and mean opposite things: one is the authority
;; saying not repealed, the other is the register never having asked.
;;
;; This is not hypothetical. The first measurement pass for this file did
;; exactly that and printed repeal=None for all eighteen laws below --
;; including 簡易生命保険法, which has been repealed since 2020. The mistake is
;; invisible because it produces the answer you expected.
;;
;; So :law/repeal-status-present? is asserted separately from the value: a
;; check that cannot find the field REFUSES instead of concluding not
;; repealed. The field being absent and the field saying None must not be the
;; same outcome.
;;
;; -- remain_in_force IS TRUE ONLY ON LAWS THAT ARE DEAD
;;
;; Counted over the entire corpus, all 9550 laws, 2026-08-27:
;;
;;   repeal_status None                 8993   remain_in_force true:   0
;;   repeal_status Repeal                414   remain_in_force true:  12
;;   repeal_status LossOfEffectiveness   105   remain_in_force true:   0
;;   repeal_status Expire                 38   remain_in_force true:   2
;;
;; Every one of the fourteen laws flagged remain_in_force is repealed or
;; expired, and EVERY LIVE LAW IN THE CORPUS HAS IT FALSE. The flag means
;; transitional provisions outlive the repeal. It does not mean in force, and
;; a check that requires it to be true admits only dead law and rejects all
;; 8993 live ones -- while reading, in code, exactly like the check you wanted.
;;
;; One of the fourteen is 母子家庭の母の就業の支援に関する特別措置法, MHLW
;; employment law, expired 2011-07-14, remain_in_force true. It is the nearest
;; miss to this repository's own subject matter and is carried below as a
;; control for that reason.
;;
;; -- NOT REPEALED IS STILL NOT THE-TEXT-YOU-GET-IS-IN-FORCE
;;
;; current_revision_status is a second, independent field, and over the same
;; 9550 laws it takes four values:
;;
;;   CurrentEnforced   8869
;;   Repeal             557
;;   PreviousEnforced   110
;;   UnEnforced          14
;;
;; 厚生年金保険法 (329AC0000000115) has repeal_status None and
;; current_revision_status PreviousEnforced: not repealed at all, yet the
;; revision the API serves is not the one in force. It is MHLW's own portfolio
;; -- an author extending this register is far more likely to reach for it
;; than for a postal-insurance statute -- so it is carried as the control for
;; this direction.
;;
;; UnEnforced is the fourth value and the one a two-valued mental model misses
;; entirely: a check written as reject-Repeal passes a revision that has not
;; come into force yet. Both fields are asserted on every statute here, and
;; both directions are held live by controls outside this repository's subject
;; matter, cited for no proposition: :law/repealed-control (Repeal),
;; :law/previous-enforced-control (None + PreviousEnforced),
;; :law/expired-control (Expire) and :law/loss-of-effectiveness-control.
;;
;; The three repeal tokens and four revision tokens above are measured over
;; the whole corpus, not assumed, and :host/repeal-tokens is asserted against
;; the controls each run so the set cannot drift without saying so.
;;
;; -- A FABRICATED LAW ID ANSWERS 200
;;
;; Same host, same fabricated id 999AC0000000999, 2026-08-27:
;;
;;   GET /api/2/law_data/999AC0000000999    -> 404, 120 B
;;   GET /api/2/laws?law_id=999AC0000000999 -> 200, total_count 0
;;
;; The first discriminates by status; the second does not. The second is the
;; one worth using -- it returns about 1.8 KB of metadata where law_data
;; returns the full text, 822 KB for 労働基準法 alone -- and switching to it
;; without moving the check off status VERIFIES EVERY FABRICATED LAW ID IN
;; THIS FILE while reporting the same all-entries-checked.
;;
;; This register uses the cheap endpoint deliberately and NEVER looks at its
;; status. Identity is total_count = 1 plus an exact law_id match, plus title,
;; number, type and promulgation date. That is strictly stronger than the
;; status check it replaces. :law/fabricated-id-control keeps the trap live by
;; asserting the fabricated id still answers 200 with an empty result, so if
;; this endpoint ever starts 404ing the note above becomes a failure rather
;; than silently over-cautious.
;;
;; -- A MISSING FILING FORM IS 48 KB OF HTML
;;
;; The 36協定 forms are .docx. A deleted one answers:
;;
;;   404, content-type text/html, 48546 B, body = the site 404 page
;;
;; Status is honest here, but the-fetch-did-not-throw and I-received-a-
;; substantial-body are both satisfied by the missing file. Every form below
;; is checked on three independent properties -- status 200, an Office or PDF
;; content-type, and the leading magic bytes -- so a dead link fails all three
;; and a content-type change fails one and is reported rather than absorbed.
;;
;; Each form also asserts that the forms page still LINKS to it. A file that
;; survives while the page that cited it stops pointing at it is an orphaned
;; citation, which is the state this register exists to prevent, and no check
;; on the file alone can see it.

[;; -- HOSTS --------------------------------------------------------------
 ;; Declared per host because they do not behave alike, and a check written
 ;; against one is wrong about the other. Measured at run time, not read off
 ;; this register.

 {:source/id :host/mhlw
  :host/name "www.mhlw.go.jp"
  :host/kind :page
  :host/charset "utf-8"
  :host/charset-in-header? false
  :host/missing-status 404
  :host/missing-title "お探しのページが見つかりません。｜厚生労働省"
  :host/missing-probe "https://www.mhlw.go.jp/zzz-no-such-page-9f3c2a1b8e/"
  :host/front-page "https://www.mhlw.go.jp/index.html"
  :host/missing-longer-than-front? false
  :host/head-404-content-length "0"
  :host/head-200-reports-body-size? false
  :source/verify :verify/page-host
  :host/note
  "Honest 404 by status and by title. Its body is smaller than the front page
   but LONGER IN TEXT than three of the pages this register depends on, so
   body-size heuristics fail in both directions here. Worse, the global
   navigation carries 労働基準法 and 労働基準監督署 -- the two obvious needles
   for this repository -- onto the missing page, while 労働安全衛生法 is absent
   from it. Needles are therefore chosen by subtracting the live 404 body, and
   the verifier redoes that subtraction each run. HEAD cannot size a body
   here: the 404 claims Content-Length 0 and then serves 48546 bytes, and a
   live page reports either no length or its compressed one, so every check
   GETs the body. Declares charset only inside the document; the header is a
   bare text/html, so bodies are decoded with a fatal decoder and an
   undecodable body REFUSES rather than reporting a missing needle."
  :host/measured "2026-08-27"}

 {:source/id :host/e-gov
  :host/name "laws.e-gov.go.jp"
  :host/kind :statute-api
  :host/api-base "https://laws.e-gov.go.jp/api/2/laws?law_id="
  :host/full-text-base "https://laws.e-gov.go.jp/api/2/law_data/"
  :host/live-repeal-token "None"
  :host/repeal-tokens ["Repeal" "Expire" "LossOfEffectiveness"]
  :host/enforced-token "CurrentEnforced"
  :host/revision-tokens ["CurrentEnforced" "Repeal" "PreviousEnforced" "UnEnforced"]
  :host/uncontrolled-revision-tokens ["UnEnforced"]
  :host/repeal-fields-in "revision_info"
  :host/fabricated-id-status 200
  :host/corpus-size 9550
  :source/verify :verify/statute-host
  :host/note
  "Two endpoints answer a fabricated law id differently: law_data 404s, the
   laws listing returns 200 with total_count 0. This register uses the listing
   endpoint -- about 450x smaller than the full text for 労働基準法 -- and
   therefore NEVER checks its status. Identity is total_count = 1 plus an
   exact law_id match. The repeal and revision fields live in revision_info,
   not law_info; read from law_info they come back nil, which is one keystroke
   from the live not-repealed token None and means the opposite. Presence of
   the field is asserted separately from its value. Being in force is
   repeal_status AND current_revision_status, because neither alone is
   sufficient, and remain_in_force is not a third opinion -- it is true only
   on repealed or expired laws. Token sets are counted over the whole corpus,
   not assumed. Three of the four revision tokens are held live by controls;
   UnEnforced deliberately is NOT, and that is named in
   :host/uncontrolled-revision-tokens rather than left silent. An UnEnforced
   law becomes CurrentEnforced on its own enforcement date, so pinning one as
   a control would schedule a false failure. The verifier prints it as
   uncontrolled on every run instead of reporting four-of-four coverage."
  :host/measured "2026-08-27"}

